Every ordinary function parameter has a name and an explicit exact value type
or parameter capability contract.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    describe |prefix String, subject String| -> String {
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    }
]

### Parameter brackets and callbacks

A signature declares the names and types of its parameters between `|` marks. A loop, option test or `catch` already knows what type of value it supplies, so its parameter brackets only need local names.

The constrained closure design follows the syntax migration. A callback parameter uses the same signature spelling as a named function. Its parameter names belong to that callback. A closure expression receives its types from the callback signature.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    row_label |
        title String,
        format_label |text String| -> String,
    | -> String {
        return format_label(title)
    }

    prefix = "Action: "
    label = row_label("Save", |caption| => [prefix, caption])
]

`format_label` takes a String and returns a String. The closure calls its incoming argument `caption` and reads `prefix` from the surrounding scope. Only `prefix` is a lexical capture. The expression after `=>` supplies the closure's result. Empty brackets mean zero arguments, so `|| => prefix` can still read `prefix`.

### Parameter forms

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    name Type
    name ~Type
    name Type = default
]

- `name Type` declares a shared parameter.
- `name ~Type` declares a mutable or exclusive parameter.
- `= default` provides a value when the caller omits that slot.
- Parameter names follow the no-shadowing rule.
- Each parameter name must be unique in the signature.

`Wire of T` and the zero/one-input `Route of ...` forms are separate parameter
contracts, not ordinary wrapper types or mutable access modes. They have no
defaults and `$` supplies no parameter mode. Their exact receiving and forwarding
rules belong to the @../wiring/#wiring-reference (Wiring reference).

Directive calls share the ordinary argument parser and slot routing. Ordinary `f()`
remains valid. Only directive syntax rejects an authored empty `()`.

### Defaults

A default belongs to the declaration.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    describe |prefix String = "item", subject String| -> String {
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    }
]

A caller can omit a defaulted parameter.

Named arguments can skip an earlier default:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    label = describe(subject = "apple")
]

A positional call cannot skip a slot in the middle. Use a named argument when
the call needs to reach a later parameter.

### Default value constraints

A parameter default must be a compile-time value.

Accepted:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    DEFAULT_PREFIX #= "item"

    describe |prefix String = DEFAULT_PREFIX, subject String| -> String {
        return [prefix, ": ", subject]
    }
]

Rejected:

- runtime bindings
- runtime function calls
- references to other parameters

Struct fields and choice payload fields can also declare typed, compile-time defaults. An omitted field uses its declared default. Supplying `none` to an optional field keeps the absence.

Supplied values use
@../language-overview/#mon-syntax (Constructor syntax). An anonymous const record requires
an initializer for every field and declares no constructable type. Its entries
are values, not parameters or defaults. See @../constants/#const-records (Const records).

A default may contain nested value construction. It cannot declare a nested
type or make an anonymous record compatible with a named parameter type.

### MON data boundary

A MON schema applies a validated default only to an omitted struct or choice payload field. A supplied `none` stays absent. A supplied nested record uses its own field rules. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format) and @../errors/#options (Options).
